<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zbruch River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zbruch is a river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, a left tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> about 245 kilometres long, separating the historical regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>. From 1772 to 1918 it formed part of the border between the Austrian and Russian empires, and between the world wars it was the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Soviet Ukraine. The Zbruch Idol, a carved stone pillar of the pre-Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, was found in it in 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zbruch-river.jpg" alt="Zbruch River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Arts at pl.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zbrucz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
